This capture shows ICMP Echo Requests being sent out, but no Echo Replies coming back. This means this device is pinging a remote host that is not responding to ICMP — which is extremely common on the modern internet.

What can be learned from this capture?
This capture shows ICMP Echo Requests sent from my device (10.218.252.84) to a remote host (23.47.72.18). Wireshark indicates “no response found,” which means the destination host did not return ICMP Echo Replies. This behavior is normal for many internet-facing servers, which block ICMP for security reasons. The capture demonstrates how ICMP requests appear in Wireshark and highlights that the absence of replies does not necessarily indicate a network failure.
What this capture shows (packet-by-packet)
1. Source IP: 10.218.252.84
This is your device.
2. Destination IP: 23.47.72.18
This is the host you pinged.
3. Protocol: ICMP
Specifically:
•	Echo (ping) request
•	id=0x0001
•	seq=4 and seq=5
•	ttl=128
4. Info: “no response found!”
This is Wireshark saying:
“See’s my ping requests, but the target never replied.”
This is not an error — it’s normal behavior.
⭐ Why there is no response
Most public servers block ICMP for security reasons.
Reasons include:
•	Preventing network mapping
•	Preventing DDoS amplification
•	Reducing attack surface
•	Avoiding unnecessary load
So when pinging:
•	Microsoft
•	Google
•	Akamai
•	Cloudflare
•	AWS
•	Azure
•	Many CDNs
… often there’s no reply.
This capture shows exactly that.
⭐ What this means in SOC / cybersecurity terms
This is actually a valuable learning moment, because SOC analysts need to understand:
✔ Not all hosts respond to ping
A lack of ICMP reply does not mean:
•	The host is down
•	The network is broken
•	The packet was dropped
•	Your system is misconfigured
It simply means:
“The host does not allow ICMP echo replies.”
✔ ICMP is often blocked at firewalls
Many enterprise networks block:
•	ICMP Echo Reply
•	ICMP Echo Request
•	ICMP Timestamp
•	ICMP Router Discovery
This is part of defense-in-depth.
✔ ICMP is still useful for diagnostics
Even without replies, we can still:
•	See outbound ICMP
•	Confirm routing
•	Analyze TTL
•	Inspect packet structure
•	Verify interface selection
This is exactly what is happening.

